welcome to the Macmillan report I'm  Marilyn Wilkes your host and our guest  is Margaret Peters an assistant  professor of political science at Yale  University her research focuses broadly  on international political economy with  a special focus on the politics of  migration her work has appeared in  international organization world  politics and international interactions  she teaches classes on international  political economy and migration today we  talk with Professor Peters about her  recent world politics article entitled  open trade closed borders immigration in  the era of globalization  welcome professor Peters thank you so  much for having me let's speak in with  an overview of your article tell us  about it right so this article looks at  immigration policy in many different  countries over the last 200 years so  going back into the 19th century and  what we see during that time period is  we see that the 19th century was a  period of a fair amount of openness  towards immigration that's when you  think about you know all those people  coming from Europe over to the United  States are moving to other parts of the  new world and there was also a fair  amount of openness within Europe itself  what we see today as you can see and  like the recent migrant crisis in Europe  and other places is a lot more  restrictions on migration and then there  were in the past so this article looks  at you know why that is and examines  this by contrasting it to trade policy  whereby we see sort of the opposite and  trade policy you know trade is very open  today but in the past it was much more  restricted I see ok and what led you to  write the article I actually started on  this project back in graduate school  when I was a little second-year graduate  student and I just was looking at  immigration policies of interest because  it was something that was fairly under  studied in political science at the time  and found this sort of interesting  correlation that countries that had more  restricted trade policies seem to have  more open immigration policy and  vice-versa so countries that had more  open trade policy seemed to have more  restricted immigration policy and I sort  of started to wonder why why this was  the case and was there a relationship  between the two mm-hmm okay and your  your we'll talk about let's talk about  the research and and how you did your  methodology and it's kind of unique  where you've you've used data that  really hasn't been tapped before correct  so what I did for the project because  there wasn't really any data on  immigration policies over those that  long of a time period or really that was  publicly available to use I created my  own indicee of immigration policy for  these 19 different countries so they  include countries in the new world so US  Canada Australia New Zealand that we  typically think about when we think  about immigration it also includes  countries in Europe which are thought to  be sort of more newer countries of  immigration and then some of these  countries that in the global South that  get a lot of immigration like the  countries in the Persian Gulf so Saudi  Arabia and Kuwait and places like  Singapore so what I did is I collected a  huge amount of data over many years  looking at the laws that these countries  had passed on immigration and then what  I did some graduate assistants and  undergraduate assistant research  researchers I then coded this data in a  bunch of different ways thinking about  how do we think about immigration policy  and coded it Wow  it sounds like a huge undertaking so why  is it important to look at immigration  in an era of globalization well I think  you know a we're in an era of  globalization now and it's also just  this really interesting question when we  think about globalization we think about  the amount of openness there is towards  trade towards the flow of capital and  then when we look at migration there's  many more restrictions on it than there  is on these other flows and so if we  think about you know globalizing the  world and integrating the world and you  know having this one large world economy  it's sort of interesting that we don't  allow the same flows of people that we  do of goods or of money mm-hmm so has  immigration and trade policy been used  over time yeah so that's what I find in  the article is that when Trey  it's relatively more restricted you get  more open immigration policy and  vice-versa so the reason I think that  this is going on is it's going through  the actions of firms so when trade is  relatively restricted firms basically  don't find as much competition from  overseas because they've have trade  barriers that help them produce at home  and a lot of the countries that I look  at that are targets of immigration this  means that these industries like  textiles or toys or other low-skilled  Industries expand and so the firms then  lobby for more workers because they're  expanding industries there's not enough  labor and so they lobby the government  for creative numbers of workers and for  Greater immigration once trade opens a  lot of those low skill firms close so  we've seen this you know in here and say  the textile sector here in the United  States we don't really you know cheap  t-shirts we get them from China now  versus once upon a time they were  manufactured here in the United States  and as that happens as those firms  clothes they no longer need the same  number of workers and so they're not  even some of those firms aren't even  here to lobby for open immigration any  longer and other firms can use the  workers that those companies laid off  and so you just see many fewer  low-skilled jobs in the country much  many fewer firms that use that kind of  labor and because of that they just  don't they're just don't exist to lobby  on immigration any longer which allows  policy makers who are facing a lot of  constraints on immigration face a lot of  groups that don't really like  immigration to restrict it to make those  groups happy without making business  interests relatively unhappy mm-hmm so  how does trade policy actually affect  immigration policy then so it's through  this firm lobbying is what I argue is  that because you see the shrinking of  the low-skilled labor intensive sectors  in these countries so again if you think  about sectors that used to use a lot of  immigrant labor here in the United  States like since we're not far from the  main old textile mills and Lowell  Massachusetts and other places like that  that used a huge amount of immigrant  labor in the 19th century when those  industries are relatively productive  they then Lobby to  help keep immigration open whereas once  we saw a greater globalization and  greater openness to trade after World  War two and those tariff barriers came  down we've seen all those textile it  firms move out of the United States move  to try it I moved to Bangladesh move to  areas with cheaper labor and because  they don't exist any longer they're not  here to lobby the government for more  open immigration right right  so what do you conclude in the article  um so I conclude a couple different  things you know first of all it makes  this I mean this argument that this  could affect other areas besides  immigration policy so we could think  about this in the same way with maybe  environmental policy so as globalization  has allowed firms to move or their  production overseas or have been  affected by other firms from other  countries we've seen the dirtier  production move offshore and so we have  relatively cleaner firms here in the  United States and so that might mean  that it's easier to pass environmental  regulation here at home because the  dirty firms have just moved overseas or  don't exist it does mean that maybe it's  harder to pass global legislation on an  environmental policy but it could affect  environmental policy same thing with  labor policy and other social policies  it also means that unfortunately for  those who would like you know to do  comprehensive immigration reform here in  the United States who would like to have  more open immigration in Europe - maybe  deal with the refugee crisis that  they're having right now that because  you don't have the same level of  business support for immigration it  means that you don't have the same  coalition that you once did for open  immigration and because we think  businesses we tend to think that they're  relatively powerful and have ways you  know they lobby a lot they provide a lot  of campaign contributions and that sort  of thing it means that because there's  not that powerful group it means that  any those forces of anti-immigration  have relatively more power so you've  seen this most recently with the way  comprehensive immigration reform has  died again in the Senate and isn't even  being taken up in the House of  Representatives because there's no real  Pro immigration Lobby pushing for it  or not to the same extent and so  anti-immigration forces have relatively  more power so it just means that we're  probably going to see more restrictions  on immigration rather than more openness  right makes sense well thanks very much  for being here today and sharing some of  your work thank you for having me for  more information about Professor Peters  and her research please visit our  website at Yale edu McMillan report be  sure to join us again for another  episode of the McMillan report made  possible through funding from the  Whitney and Betty McMillan Center for  International and area studies at Yale  you
 

